  "query_preview": "..The upper low that has brought showers to the area\nthe last couple of days is now centered over southwest Wyoming as\nit continues to exit the area. Behind this low, showers have\nmostly dissipated over Utah this evening. A transient shortwave\nridge will cross the area late tonight through Monday morning\nbefore southwesterly flow aloft increases ahead of the next\ntrough which is currently noted over the PacNW coast. Fairly \nbenign weather can be expected into early Monday evening. \nTemperatures on Monday will trend warmer, rising to near to \nslightly above normal across northern Utah but remaining below \nnormal across the south.\n\nThe upstream trough will end up grazing northern Utah late Monday\nevening through Monday night. This will push a relatively shallow\ncold front into northern Utah. A quick round of showers is\nexpected to accompany this front. The latest high-res models are \nnot as enthusiastic about precipitation compared to the global \nmodels. For example, the 75th percentile of the REFS is only \nshowing 0.15-0.3 inches in the Bear River Range, with generally \nless than 0.1 inches in the adjacent valleys and no measurable \nprecipitation south of around Ogden. Meanwhile, the NBM 50th \npercentile has slightly higher amounts than that. Suspect the NBM \nwill trend downward with the global deterministics trending \ntowards a weaker cold front. With snow levels falling as low as\n6500 feet near the Idaho border, some light snow accumulations \ncan be expected over the higher terrain.\n\nCooler temperatures can be expected again on Tuesday behind the\ncold front, with northern Utah trending back to slightly below \nnormal temperatures. Otherwise, any precipitation from the cold \nfront will clear the area by around sunrise, leading to mostly \nclear skies during the morning through late afternoon. \nSouthwesterly flow aloft will quickly redevelop as a second, \nweakening trough swings into the northern Great Basin. This \ntrough will then eject across northern Utah Tuesday night, \nbringing another potential round of light precipitation to \nnorthern Utah. Deterministic models have this second trough \ntrending slightly stronger, which falls in line with the first \ntrough trending slightly weaker.\n\nThereafter, models are still pointing to an active pattern for the\nweekend into potentially early next week as a strengthening storm\nsystem over the PacNW phases with a second system originating\nfarther west that taps into a plume of moisture. Latest global\ndeterministics are introducing greater timing uncertainties in \nthat the 18z GFS does not bring any precipitation into Utah until \nSaturday evening, compared to earlier runs and the EC which have \nprecipitation beginning as early as Friday. Nevertheless, Sunday \nlooks to be the wettest day at this time. However, there remains \nsome model spread regarding how the large-scale pattern evolves \nthrough the weekend and how quickly the storm exits the area.",
